== 1. Fermat primes and rigid circulant tournaments
   Claim: p is a Fermat prime  <=>  every circulant tournament on Z_p has Aut = Z_p.
   p =  3: Aut orders of all 2 circulant tournaments (brute force): [3]
   p =  5: Aut orders of all 4 circulant tournaments (brute force): [5]
   p =  7: Aut orders of all 8 circulant tournaments (brute force): [7, 21]
   p = 11: Aut orders of all 32 circulant tournaments (brute force): [11, 55]
   primes p < 400 with p - 1 a power of 2 (all circulant tournaments rigid): [3, 5, 17, 257]
   multiplier stabilizers checked exhaustively for p <= 31: rigid exactly at p = 3, 5, 17: True
   number of rotational tournaments up to isomorphism at a Fermat prime p (free Z_(p-1) action): 2^((p-1)/2)/(p-1): {3: 1, 5: 1, 17: 16, 257: '2^120', 65537: '2^32752'}

== 2. Wieferich primes: circulant tournaments on Z_(p^2) invariant under g = the odd-order part of <2>
   (g = 2^(ord_p(2)/d), d = odd part of ord_p(2); invariant tournaments exist because <g> has odd order, so -1 is not in <g>)
   p =     3: ord_p(2) =     2, odd part d =     1, ord_(p^2)(g) =        3;  invariant tournaments on Z_(p^2): 2^2  (2^2 unless Wieferich);  Wieferich: False
   p =     7: ord_p(2) =     3, odd part d =     3, ord_(p^2)(g) =       21;  invariant tournaments on Z_(p^2): 2^2  (2^2 unless Wieferich);  Wieferich: False
   p =    31: ord_p(2) =     5, odd part d =     5, ord_(p^2)(g) =      155;  invariant tournaments on Z_(p^2): 2^6  (2^6 unless Wieferich);  Wieferich: False
   p =   127: ord_p(2) =     7, odd part d =     7, ord_(p^2)(g) =      889;  invariant tournaments on Z_(p^2): 2^18  (2^18 unless Wieferich);  Wieferich: False
   p =  1093: ord_p(2) =   364, odd part d =    91, ord_(p^2)(g) =       91;  invariant tournaments on Z_(p^2): 2^6564  (2^12 unless Wieferich);  Wieferich: True
   p =  3511: ord_p(2) =  1755, odd part d =  1755, ord_(p^2)(g) =     1755;  invariant tournaments on Z_(p^2): 2^3512  (2^2 unless Wieferich);  Wieferich: True
   below 4000 the exponent jumps exactly at the Wieferich primes 1093 and 3511: True
   3511: ord_3511(2) = 1755 = (p-1)/2, p = 7 mod 8, p = 3 mod 4: <2> = the squares, so the doubling-invariant circulant tournaments on Z_3511 are the Paley tournament and its reverse (as <2> = {1,2,4} gives P_7 at p = 7)
   1093 = (3^7 - 1)/2: True;  ord_1093(3) = 7;  ord_1093(2) = 364 = 4 * 91, 91 = 7 * 13;  1093 mod 21 = 1, 3511 mod 21 = 4 (both in <2> mod 21 = [1, 2, 4, 8, 11, 16], i.e. both split in Q(sqrt(-7)))

== 3. Hamiltonian paths of the Paley tournaments P_p (p = 3 mod 4)
   p =  3: H(P_p) = 3  (= 3 * 1; |Aut| = p(p-1)/2 divides H: True);  H/p mod p = 1, H/|Aut| mod p = 1;  0.6 s
   p =  7: H(P_p) = 189  (= 21 * 9; |Aut| = p(p-1)/2 divides H: True);  H/p mod p = 6, H/|Aut| mod p = 2;  0.0 s
   p = 11: H(P_p) = 95095  (= 55 * 1729; |Aut| = p(p-1)/2 divides H: True);  H/p mod p = 10, H/|Aut| mod p = 2;  0.0 s
   p = 19: H(P_p) = 1172695746915  (= 171 * 6857869865; |Aut| = p(p-1)/2 divides H: True);  H/p mod p = 17, H/|Aut| mod p = 4;  0.1 s
   p = 23: H(P_p) = 15760206976379349  (= 253 * 62293308207033; |Aut| = p(p-1)/2 divides H: True);  H/p mod p = 16, H/|Aut| mod p = 14;  2.7 s
   orbit counts H/|Aut| mod 8 for the Paley primes: {3: 1, 7: 1, 11: 1, 19: 1, 23: 1}

