     p = 7:  2 connection sets with |Aut| =  21, H =     189, H/|Aut| = 9 = 1 mod 8
     p = 11: 10 connection sets with |Aut| =  11, H =   92411, H/|Aut| = 8401 = 1 mod 8
     p = 11: 10 connection sets with |Aut| =  11, H =   93027, H/|Aut| = 8457 = 1 mod 8
     p = 11: 10 connection sets with |Aut| =  11, H =   93467, H/|Aut| = 8497 = 1 mod 8
     p = 11:  2 connection sets with |Aut| =  55, H =   95095, H/|Aut| = 1729 = 1 mod 8

   all circulant tournaments on Z_p: residues of the orbit count H/|Aut| (|Aut| = p |M|) mod 8, by p mod 4
     p =  3 (p = 3 mod 4): 2 connection sets, residues of H/|Aut| mod 8: {1: 2}
     p =  5 (p = 1 mod 4): 4 connection sets, residues of H/|Aut| mod 8: {3: 4}
     p =  7 (p = 3 mod 4): 8 connection sets, residues of H/|Aut| mod 8: {1: 8}
     p = 11 (p = 3 mod 4): 32 connection sets, residues of H/|Aut| mod 8: {1: 32}
     p = 13 (p = 1 mod 4): 64 connection sets, residues of H/|Aut| mod 8: {1: 12, 3: 12, 5: 28, 7: 12}
     p = 17 (p = 1 mod 4): 256 connection sets, residues of H/|Aut| mod 8: {1: 112, 3: 32, 5: 64, 7: 48}
     p = 19 (p = 3 mod 4): 512 connection sets, residues of H/|Aut| mod 8: {1: 128, 3: 126, 5: 96, 7: 162}
     -> the guess "every circulant on Z_p with p = 3 mod 4 has orbit count 1 mod 8" holds at p = 3, 7, 11 and is REFUTED at p = 19

   control: the doubly regular tournaments of the skew-Hadamard doubling tower (gilbreath_fermat_tower_20260926_tournaments.py;
   |Aut(T_4)| = 21 there, not vertex-transitive).  Aut acts freely on Hamiltonian paths of any tournament.
     T_3 (n = 7): H = 189, |Aut| = 21, H/|Aut| = 9 = 1 mod 8
     T_4 (n = 15): H = 198335025, |Aut| = 21, H/|Aut| = 9444525 = 5 mod 8

   the Paley tournament on GF(27) = F_3[x]/(x^3 - x - 1): H mod 8424 = 8 * 1053 (|Aut| = 27 * 13 * 3 = 1053)
     H(P_27) mod 8424 = 3159;  divisible by 1053: True;  orbit count under the full Aut (with Frobenius): H/1053 = 3 mod 8;  under the affine group x -> a x + b (a a square, order 351): H/351 = 1 mod 8;  60 s

== 4. Paley graphs on Fermat primes: clique numbers
   P_5: clique number 2 (= independence number, self-complementary), so R(3,3) > 5;  0.0 s
   P_13: clique number 3 (= independence number, self-complementary), so R(4,4) > 13;  0.0 s
   P_17: clique number 3 (= independence number, self-complementary), so R(4,4) > 17;  0.0 s
   P_29: clique number 4 (= independence number, self-complementary), so R(5,5) > 29;  0.0 s
   P_37: clique number 4 (= independence number, self-complementary), so R(5,5) > 37;  0.0 s
   P_41: clique number 5 (= independence number, self-complementary), so R(6,6) > 41;  0.0 s
   P_257: clique number 7 (= independence number, self-complementary), so R(8,8) > 257;  0.1 s
   R(3,3) = 6 and R(4,4) = 18: the Paley graphs on the Fermat primes 5 and 17 are the extremal graphs (classical; uniqueness cited, not re-checked)

== 5. Triangle groups (2,3,k), perfect 6 and primary pseudoperfect 42
   (2,3,3): orbifold Euler characteristic 1/6 -> spherical, |group| = 2/chi = 12
   (2,3,4): orbifold Euler characteristic 1/12 -> spherical, |group| = 2/chi = 24
   (2,3,5): orbifold Euler characteristic 1/30 -> spherical, |group| = 2/chi = 60
   (2,3,6): orbifold Euler characteristic 0 -> Euclidean
   (2,3,7): orbifold Euler characteristic -1/42 -> hyperbolic, Hurwitz: |Aut| <= (2g-2)/|chi| = 84(g-1)
   (2,3,8): orbifold Euler characteristic -1/24 -> hyperbolic, Hurwitz: |Aut| <= (2g-2)/|chi| = 48(g-1)
   primary pseudoperfect numbers n < 60000 (sum_{p|n} 1/p + 1/n = 1): [2, 6, 42, 1806, 47058]
   chi(2,3,7) = -1/42 with 42 = 2*3*7 primary pseudoperfect;  chi(2,3,6) = 0 with 6 perfect;  Sylvester 2, 3, 7, 43, 1807 = 13 * 139
   sigma(n)/n = sum_{d | n} 1/d for every n, so sigma(n)/n - 2 = sum_{d | n, d > 1} 1/d - 1 is a tautology;  the triangle reading
   needs exactly three divisors above 1, i.e. tau(n) = 4 (n = pq or p^3):
   n =  6 (tau = 4): divisors > 1 [2, 3, 6], triangle excess/pi = 0, sigma(n)/n - 2 = 0, equal: True
   n = 15 (tau = 4): divisors > 1 [3, 5, 15], triangle excess/pi = -2/5, sigma(n)/n - 2 = -2/5, equal: True
   n = 21 (tau = 4): divisors > 1 [3, 7, 21], triangle excess/pi = -10/21, sigma(n)/n - 2 = -10/21, equal: True
   n = 35 (tau = 4): divisors > 1 [5, 7, 35], triangle excess/pi = -22/35, sigma(n)/n - 2 = -22/35, equal: True
   n = 77 (tau = 4): divisors > 1 [7, 11, 77], triangle excess/pi = -58/77, sigma(n)/n - 2 = -58/77, equal: True
   n =  4 (tau = 3): divisors > 1 [2, 4], not a triangle (excluded)
   n =  8 (tau = 4): divisors > 1 [2, 4, 8], triangle excess/pi = -1/8, sigma(n)/n - 2 = -1/8, equal: True
   n = 27 (tau = 4): divisors > 1 [3, 9, 27], triangle excess/pi = -14/27, sigma(n)/n - 2 = -14/27, equal: True
   tau(n) = 4, n < 10^5: abundant [], perfect [6] (only 6 = 2*3 <-> (2,3,6); no spherical case)

